$C_BODY = "The Castaic Lake Water Agency was the Santa Clarita Valley's wholesale water agency, which bought State Water Project water for the valley's retail water suppliers, governed by an elected board: a director at large and directors for three divisions. Valley voters chose on 24 April 1962 to join the State Water Project, which brought the Upper Santa Clara Valley Water Agency into being; it was renamed the Castaic Lake Water Agency in 1970.[1]\n\nIt became a retailer too. The private Santa Clarita Water Company had grown from the Bonelli family's Bouquet Canyon Water Company of 1949 and Solemint Water Company of 1956. The agency took all its stock for \$63 million by a condemnation judgment entered on the owners' agreement on 3 September 1999, and ran its service as the agency's Santa Clarita Water Division, a retail division with no separate existence, serving parts of the City and the unincorporated land of Saugus, Canyon Country and West Newhall.[2][3][4] In December 2012 it bought all the stock of the Valencia Water Company from Newhall Land, and kept it as a separate company.[5]\n\nOn 1 January 2018 Senate Bill 634 reorganized the agency, with the Newhall County Water District, into the Santa Clarita Valley Water Agency, their successor. Its Santa Clarita Water Division passed to the new agency, which keeps a division of that name, and the act directed the new agency to dissolve the Valencia Water Company.[6][4]";
